1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,848
The Priority Action Approach organizes attention. Start with safety,
identify the problem that could harm the patient first, act on it, and
then continue gathering information. Saying an acronym without
examining or intervening does not complete an assessment.

2
00:00:16,848 --> 00:00:31,824
For an apparently well person, conversation and rapport can precede
detailed examination. An unwell person needs a rapid formal survey.
Either way, your first impression is provisional and must be tested.

3
00:00:31,824 --> 00:00:42,648
A partner may obtain measurements while you assess another priority.
Name the task and confirm its result; parallel work is not the same as
leaving a step to chance.

4
00:00:42,648 --> 00:00:54,864
This lesson follows the seven sections of school 7A. The laboratory
timing targets are practice expectations; current service guidance and
the patient determine clinical actions.

5
00:00:54,864 --> 00:01:09,096
Before approaching, scan the area and your route back to the
ambulance. Ask what could change once you are committed beside the
patient: a closing door, an aggressive bystander, traffic or an
unstable surface.

6
00:01:09,096 --> 00:01:21,072
Count patients rather than accepting the dispatch count as final.
Identify whether fire, police, rescue or an additional clinical crew
is needed, and request them early.

7
00:01:21,072 --> 00:01:36,024
Choose precautions from the actual exposure risk before contact.
Gloves do not protect against every hazard and do not replace hand
hygiene. An uncontrolled hazard is a reason to remain at a safe
distance, not to hurry through it.

8
00:01:36,024 --> 00:01:45,840
Recheck scene safety during treatment and departure. A safe arrival
does not guarantee a safe move down the stairs or a safe return
through the same doorway.

9
00:01:45,840 --> 00:02:00,432
Mechanism of injury describes the forces or event associated with
trauma. Nature of illness describes the medical problem being
investigated. The two can coexist: a medical collapse can cause a
traumatic fall.

10
00:02:00,432 --> 00:02:12,336
Medication packaging suggests questions, not proof that a drug was
taken today. A walker suggests asking about baseline mobility, not
assuming that current weakness is usual.

11
00:02:12,336 --> 00:02:23,856
Ask a witness what they saw, when the patient was last functioning
normally and what changed. Attribute the account to that witness and
retain uncertainty about unobserved events.

12
00:02:23,856 --> 00:02:34,272
Several occupants with headache and nausea should redirect attention
to a shared environmental exposure. Do not enter an unsafe building to
obtain a better history.

13
00:02:34,272 --> 00:02:44,568
Look before touching. A person unable to finish a sentence, slumping
with poor tone or becoming confused is not made safe by a reassuring
dispatch label.

14
00:02:44,568 --> 00:02:56,808
If there is life-threatening external hemorrhage, arrange immediate
control before the formal survey continues. Do not wait until the
letter C simply because a memorized sequence places it later.

15
00:02:56,808 --> 00:03:10,968
An unconscious patient requires breathing and circulation to be
assessed together, with the arrest pathway activated when indicated. A
slow, rigid airway-first recital must not delay chest compressions.

16
00:03:10,968 --> 00:03:24,264
State your concern aloud and delegate. For example: this patient looks
critically unwell; bring the resuscitation equipment and prepare for
urgent movement while we assess immediate threats.

17
00:03:24,264 --> 00:03:39,240
Introduce yourself if the person can engage, and assess spontaneous
alertness and response to voice. AVPU is a rapid coarse description:
alert, voice, pressure or pain response, and unresponsive.

18
00:03:39,240 --> 00:03:50,952
A detailed Glasgow Coma Scale assessment comes next when appropriate.
Report its components rather than guessing a total from a greeting or
labelling every confused person intoxicated.

19
00:03:50,952 --> 00:04:06,264
When trauma or the mechanism raises spinal concern, delegate suitable
movement control. Opening an obstructed airway and effective
resuscitation remain priorities; do not maintain a dangerous position
just to avoid all movement.

20
00:04:06,264 --> 00:04:18,960
Do not practise painful stimuli on classmates. In simulation,
verbalize the assessment and use the instructor-approved response cue;
practical technique requires direct supervision.

21
00:04:18,960 --> 00:04:33,504
A speaking patient demonstrates airflow at that moment, but this does
not guarantee continuing patency. Listen for changing voice, gurgling,
snoring or stridor, and look for blood, vomit or swelling.

22
00:04:33,504 --> 00:04:45,816
Use the airway-opening approach appropriate to the clinical situation
and your training. Clear obstructing material with approved methods
and suction when indicated; do not perform blind finger sweeps.

23
00:04:45,816 --> 00:04:59,304
An OPA or NPA is considered according to responsiveness, protective
reflexes, anatomy and contraindications. Reduced consciousness alone
does not authorize either device without assessment.

24
00:04:59,304 --> 00:05:09,960
Immediately reassess whether air moves. Preparing a device, inserting
it or announcing airway clear is not equivalent to demonstrating a
functioning airway.

25
00:05:09,960 --> 00:05:21,552
Observe actual chest rise and respiratory effort. Six shallow breaths
per minute in an unresponsive adult is a ventilation problem even
before a monitor produces a number.

26
00:05:21,552 --> 00:05:35,496
Supplemental oxygen can improve saturation without correcting carbon
dioxide retention. If ventilation is inadequate, support it with the
trained bag-mask technique and reassess chest rise and air movement.

27
00:05:35,496 --> 00:05:48,336
The school uses respiratory-rate extremes as red flags. An apparently
ordinary rate with tiny tidal volumes, exhaustion or irregular pauses
can still represent respiratory failure.

28
00:05:48,336 --> 00:06:01,728
Give indicated oxygen without delaying care to obtain a room-air
reading. Device choice and oxygen targets must follow the relevant
current clinical guidance, not an automatic maximum for every patient.

29
00:06:01,728 --> 00:06:12,744
Palpate an appropriate pulse and correlate it with the patient, not
only the displayed heart rate. Electrical activity on a monitor does
not prove mechanical circulation.

30
00:06:12,744 --> 00:06:23,280
Major visible hemorrhage should already be controlled. Recheck the
effectiveness of pressure, packing or a tourniquet as appropriate to
training, especially after movement.

31
00:06:23,280 --> 00:06:35,112
A palpable radial pulse cannot be converted into a reliable systolic
pressure. Nor does it prove adequate brain perfusion; compare
alertness, skin and serial measurements.

32
00:06:35,112 --> 00:06:48,288
Shock means inadequate tissue perfusion, not simply low blood
pressure. Compensatory vasoconstriction and tachycardia can preserve
the pressure while the patient is becoming more unwell.

33
00:06:48,288 --> 00:06:58,368
The rapid body survey is a brief search for important hidden injury,
tailored to the presentation. It is not the detailed secondary head-
to-toe examination.

34
00:06:58,368 --> 00:07:24,144
Inspect and gently assess systematically, including blood on your
gloves and concealed surfaces when safe access is possible. The school
also names subcutaneous emphysema: air in tissue under the skin,
potentially felt as crackling around the chest or neck. Report the
finding and assess related breathing or injury concerns; a rapid sweep
does not exclude internal injury.

35
00:07:24,144 --> 00:07:38,184
Do not repeatedly spring or rock a suspected injured pelvis to prove
instability. Avoid unnecessary manipulation of fractures; arrange
appropriate stabilization and movement with trained supervision.

36
00:07:38,184 --> 00:07:52,344
The school describes an approximately thirty-second rapid survey and a
primary survey within two minutes when no intervention is needed.
These are fluency targets, not reasons to omit a threat or delay
necessary care.

37
00:07:52,344 --> 00:08:08,352
In school 7A, SOAPPP means skin, oxygen, airway adjunct, pupils,
pressure or bleeding, and position or packaging. Use it as a final
omission check, not a second primary survey performed from the
beginning.

38
00:08:08,352 --> 00:08:22,032
Ask whether each intervention is needed and effective now. Oxygen may
need titration; an adjunct may become inappropriate if protective
reflexes return; bleeding can recur after movement.

39
00:08:22,032 --> 00:08:34,920
Packaging includes warmth, secure equipment, suitable positioning and
a movement plan. The priority is safe, effective departure rather than
making every strap and monitor lead look perfect first.

40
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:47,880
The current saved provincial exam list also names bandaging, pelvic
binding and blankets. Learn both source-specific lists instead of
assuming the school acronym reproduces every licensing item.

41
00:08:47,880 --> 00:08:59,952
After initial life threats are addressed, state whether this is a
rapid-transport-category patient in the school framework. The decision
is based on the whole presentation and can change.

42
00:08:59,952 --> 00:09:11,328
A major injury can need urgent definitive care despite a normal
initial pressure. Conversely, a medical emergency can be unstable
without a traumatic mechanism.

43
00:09:11,328 --> 00:09:22,800
Give the reason and the operational consequence: urgent transport
because of poor perfusion; essential hemorrhage and airway work now;
remaining history during safe conveyance.

44
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:32,040
The destination follows the relevant regional clinical pathway and
resources, not simply the nearest building or the existence of a
particular acronym.

45
00:09:32,040 --> 00:09:45,768
School 7A highlights penetrating injuries to the head, neck, torso or
limbs proximal to the elbow or knee. The concern is damage to major
structures and bleeding not visible from the wound size.

46
00:09:45,768 --> 00:09:59,040
Chest-wall instability, pelvic injury and two or more proximal long-
bone fractures can represent serious injury and concealed blood loss.
Normal initial measurements do not cancel those risks.

47
00:09:59,040 --> 00:10:10,248
A crushed, degloved, mangled or pulseless limb and an amputation
proximal to the wrist or ankle require urgent attention to limb and
systemic threats.

48
00:10:10,248 --> 00:10:24,312
The provincial examination document adds other unstable findings,
including new neurological deficits and major burns. A source list is
not a reason to ignore an equally serious finding absent from a school
slide.

49
00:10:24,312 --> 00:10:39,648
The school gives GCS below fourteen and systolic pressure below ninety
as numeric RTC cues. Its respiratory examples include fewer than ten
or more than thirty breaths per minute, with inconsistent wording
about accompanying shock.

50
00:10:39,648 --> 00:10:51,672
Do not interpret that ambiguity as permission to wait for shock before
treating severe respiratory distress. Assess adequacy, effort,
mentation and the patient's trajectory.

51
00:10:51,672 --> 00:11:05,472
The licensing document treats altered consciousness, airway
difficulty, respiratory distress and several time-critical medical
syndromes as unstable findings without requiring those exact school
thresholds.

52
00:11:05,472 --> 00:11:16,464
A change from a personal baseline matters. New confusion with a
pressure of one hundred and twenty is still concerning, and shock can
be present before a pressure falls below ninety.

53
00:11:16,464 --> 00:11:31,296
Learn the school mechanism examples as assigned: an adult fall above
twenty feet, and a child fall above ten feet or roughly two to three
body heights. These are school triage cues, not a guarantee of safety
below them.

54
00:11:31,296 --> 00:11:58,704
Vehicle ejection, a death in the same compartment and major intrusion
indicate substantial energy. School 7A names more than twelve inches
of intrusion at the occupant site and a motorcycle crash above thirty
kilometres per hour. Preserve those school examples alongside the
provincial values on the next segment. Ask where the patient sat and
how the body moved; a photograph alone does not establish the injury.

55
00:11:58,704 --> 00:12:13,512
The saved provincial guideline specifies adult free fall above six
metres, intrusion above point three metres on the occupant side or
point five metres elsewhere, and high-risk road impacts. Keep units
attached to the numbers.

56
00:12:13,512 --> 00:12:26,136
A low-energy fall in a frail older adult can still cause major injury
or follow a serious medical event. Mechanism supplements physiology
and examination; it never replaces them.

57
00:12:26,136 --> 00:12:44,808
School 7A repeatedly uses fifteen minutes as its RTC scene-time
benchmark. Do not call it an absolute BC legal rule: current BCEHS A01
and A02 emphasize meaningful interventions and minimizing scene time,
not that universal deadline.

58
00:12:44,808 --> 00:12:56,424
Fifteen minutes is not permission to remain until the timer expires.
If essential support and safe packaging are complete, proceed
according to urgency and the transport plan.

59
00:12:56,424 --> 00:13:08,784
Neither should a deadline cause unsafe extrication or abandonment of
essential resuscitation. Communicate barriers, request help and
document the actual reason for unavoidable delay.

60
00:13:08,784 --> 00:13:21,384
Do not blindly defer every vital sign. A delegated measurement that
immediately changes management may be meaningful now; a long
nonessential task should not postpone definitive care.

61
00:13:21,384 --> 00:13:35,472
Before lifting, decide how the patient can be moved with the least
additional risk. Consider consciousness, ventilation, pain, suspected
injuries, body size, route restrictions and available personnel.

62
00:13:35,472 --> 00:13:48,624
Assign a movement leader, agree on commands and specify when anyone
should stop. Use the approved device within its limits; enthusiasm
cannot replace adequate staff or equipment.

63
00:13:48,624 --> 00:14:00,336
Choose a position the patient can tolerate without worsening the
clinical threat. A standard diagram does not override vomiting,
respiratory distress or the need for resuscitation.

64
00:14:00,336 --> 00:14:13,560
Reassess immediately after movement: airway, ventilation, perfusion,
symptoms, bleeding control and device function. A previously effective
treatment can fail during loading.

65
00:14:13,560 --> 00:14:25,128
For the school prone-patient exercise, begin with what is actually
known: a person is face down and not responding. Do not invent a fall
or intoxication as the established cause.

66
00:14:25,128 --> 00:14:39,336
After safety, rapidly evaluate response and accessible signs of
breathing and circulation. Obtain help and arrange a coordinated move
when needed to open the airway, assess properly or resuscitate.

67
00:14:39,336 --> 00:14:54,096
In the supervised station, identify the leader, protect alignment as
practicable, coordinate shoulders, trunk and pelvis, and stop for a
problem. Do not independently improvise a one-person roll from this
text.

68
00:14:54,096 --> 00:15:06,480
Once positioned, immediately reassess and treat findings. A glucose
measurement and neurological examination are important, but neither
precedes needed ventilation or CPR.

69
00:15:06,480 --> 00:15:17,160
Start with an open account: what happened today and what is different
from usual? Then use SAMPLE to check omissions rather than
interrupting the story after every word.

70
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:31,272
For allergies, record the substance and reaction, not only yes or no.
For medications, ask about actual use, dose timing, recent changes,
nonprescription products and substances relevant to the presentation.

71
00:15:31,272 --> 00:15:43,320
Past history should be pertinent, while last oral intake and events
may explain risk or guide later care. Clarify whether the last meal
was missed rather than assuming a normal schedule.

72
00:15:43,320 --> 00:15:53,376
When the patient cannot answer, use a witness or caregiver and label
the source. Not known is different from no history, no medication or
no allergy.

73
00:15:53,376 --> 00:16:03,912
Ask when the symptom began, whether onset was sudden or gradual, and
what the patient was doing. Distinguish onset time from the time the
ambulance was called.

74
00:16:03,912 --> 00:16:16,512
Ask what worsens or relieves it, what it feels like, where it is and
whether it travels. Avoid suggesting the answer: tell me what it feels
like is better than it is just a sharp pain, right?

75
00:16:16,512 --> 00:16:27,576
Use an appropriate severity scale, then ask how the symptom changed
over time and what activity it now prevents. A numeric score alone
does not describe the clinical problem.

76
00:16:27,576 --> 00:16:41,952
A painless or low-pain presentation does not exclude serious disease.
Link symptom characteristics with examination, risk context and
associated findings rather than using one reassuring answer to stop.

77
00:16:41,952 --> 00:16:51,936
School 7A uses PASTMED for dyspnea. Ask what provoked the problem and
whether it is progressing, then ask about chest pain, cough and
sputum.

78
00:16:51,936 --> 00:17:03,480
Clarify timing and any trauma. Ask which medication was actually used,
when it was taken and whether it helped; an inhaler nearby does not
establish the response.

79
00:17:03,480 --> 00:17:17,328
Compare current exercise tolerance with the patient's own baseline,
including usual oxygen use and prior diagnosis. Ask about fever,
allergens, leg symptoms or other relevant associated concerns.

80
00:17:17,328 --> 00:17:29,448
Ask short questions if speech is limited, and obtain collateral
history while another clinician supports breathing. Finishing the
mnemonic is never more important than adequate ventilation.

81
00:17:29,448 --> 00:17:40,944
A functional inquiry is a targeted review of related systems, not just
asking how well the patient walks. It can reveal an associated symptom
the patient did not think to mention.

82
00:17:40,944 --> 00:17:53,616
With abdominal pain, ask about vomiting or stool blood, urinary
symptoms, pregnancy possibility and previous surgery as relevant. Keep
questions respectful and privacy appropriate.

83
00:17:53,616 --> 00:18:08,784
With altered consciousness, establish baseline and last-known-well,
investigate hypoxia and glucose, and consider trauma, stroke,
seizures, infection and toxic exposure. Do not close on one scene
clue.

84
00:18:08,784 --> 00:18:20,904
A red flag changes urgency; a negative answer changes probability but
does not prove absence. Explain which answer would change your next
examination or transport decision.

85
00:18:20,904 --> 00:18:33,024
A focused examination is not permission to inspect only the painful
spot. For an injured wrist after a collapse, the collapse may require
a broader assessment than the wrist itself.

86
00:18:33,024 --> 00:18:46,200
Use a systematic complete examination when major trauma, altered
mentation or uncertainty makes hidden findings plausible. Adapt the
depth and location of the exam to transport urgency.

87
00:18:46,200 --> 00:19:00,456
Explain what you need to examine and why, obtain appropriate
permission, and limit exposure. Observe, feel and listen as relevant;
compare bilateral findings and distal function when injury suggests
it.

88
00:19:00,456 --> 00:19:09,720
An examination should test a clinical question. State what a finding
supports, what it cannot exclude and what you still need to
investigate.

89
00:19:09,720 --> 00:19:21,768
Repeat the primary priorities, compare the patient with the previous
examination and recheck each intervention. Ask what changed, what
should have changed and whether anything became worse.

90
00:19:21,768 --> 00:19:44,592
School 7A teaches five-minute unstable and fifteen-minute stable
reassessment. Older A01 uses a fifteen-minute minimum, but the new
documentation section has selected thirty-minute, fifteen-minute and
more frequent categories. The monitoring lesson explains this
publication conflict; confirm the applicable service instruction.

91
00:19:44,592 --> 00:19:56,472
These intervals are not waiting periods. New confusion, pain, a
breathing change, an intervention, movement or an equipment alarm
triggers reassessment immediately.

92
00:19:56,472 --> 00:20:06,408
Record actual values and their times. A line saying stable cannot show
a falling pressure, rising respiratory rate or failed response to
treatment.

93
00:20:06,408 --> 00:20:17,424
An initial explanation is useful only while it fits the evidence.
Alcohol on the scene does not exclude hypoglycemia, head injury,
hypoxia or stroke.

94
00:20:17,424 --> 00:20:29,664
Actively seek a finding that could contradict your leading impression.
If you believe anxiety explains breathlessness, ask what would suggest
a dangerous respiratory or circulatory cause instead.

95
00:20:29,664 --> 00:20:40,728
Task fixation can look like technical excellence: a perfect splint
while perfusion worsens. Periodically ask whether the current task
still deserves the team's time.

96
00:20:40,728 --> 00:20:51,792
A cognitive pause is brief and targeted. It does not stop needed
resuscitation; it prevents an untested assumption from controlling
every later decision.

97
00:20:51,792 --> 00:21:04,608
This original practice variant mirrors the type of reasoning required
in the school older-fall case; it is not a school assessment answer.
Ask whether illness caused the fall and what happened afterward.

98
00:21:04,608 --> 00:21:20,592
A caregiver reports new confusion, while the patient reports hip pain.
Both accounts matter. Establish baseline, head impact, anticoagulant
use, preceding dizziness, infection symptoms and time on the floor.

99
00:21:20,592 --> 00:21:35,832
Check immediate threats, actual vital signs, glucose and relevant
neurological and injury findings. Do not force weight bearing to prove
a fracture, and do not let a low total NEWS2 cancel a significant red
flag.

100
00:21:35,832 --> 00:21:48,264
State the movement and transport plan, reassess after packaging, and
report uncertainty. The receiving clinician needs the new confusion
and trajectory, not only the label mechanical fall.

101
00:21:48,264 --> 00:22:01,056
Record scene context, initial findings and the transport decision with
its reason. Add a timed history, examination and measured observations
rather than simply marking every system normal.

102
00:22:01,056 --> 00:22:15,432
Keep a patient statement distinct from observation: the patient
reports dizziness; you observe pallor. A working impression is
legitimate when labelled and supported, not presented as an
established diagnosis.

103
00:22:15,432 --> 00:22:24,384
Pair actions with response and time. Document what could not be
obtained and why; never invent a number to make the record complete.

104
00:22:24,384 --> 00:22:37,056
End with the receiving clinician, time, important unresolved concerns
and transfer acceptance. Documentation and ongoing reassessment
continue while waiting for formal handover.

105
00:22:37,056 --> 00:22:53,400
NEWS2 structures adult observations using respiratory rate, oxygen
saturation, systolic blood pressure, pulse, temperature and
consciousness or new confusion. Learn the components and time of each
observation, not only the total.

106
00:22:53,400 --> 00:23:21,384
Record supplemental oxygen status, device and flow. The saved school
7A narrative says one oxygen point, but the Royal College of
Physicians NEWS2 chart adds two. Use the correct chart rather than
combining the two versions, and ask the instructor to clarify the
school discrepancy. Select the saturation scale and treatment target
under the applicable clinical guidance; a score does not choose an
oxygen target.

107
00:23:21,384 --> 00:23:42,336
NEWS2 is not a pediatric score. School 7A introduces PEWS through
behavior, cardiovascular status and respiratory effort. Children need
the current age-specific PEWS reference taught in the school pediatric
block. Do not apply adult NEWS2 thresholds or assume one age band fits
every child.

108
00:23:42,336 --> 00:23:59,064
Use scores to summarize a trend and communicate concern, never to
diagnose, rule out deterioration or cancel a red flag. Reassess and
escalate a significant change under current service direction even
when a total appears reassuring.

109
00:23:59,064 --> 00:24:13,224
First complete the clinically necessary assessment and write down what
was actually observed or measured, with its time. A score is a way to
organize information; calculating one must not delay an immediate
response.

110
00:24:13,224 --> 00:24:29,352
Choose the tool for the patient group and use its exact approved
chart. In this school module, NEWS2 is the adult example and PEWS is
the pediatric example. Do not apply adult thresholds to a child or
combine different versions into a new chart.

111
00:24:29,352 --> 00:24:47,832
Follow the selected chart’s age band, units, oxygen conditions and
parameter definitions. Enter each required observation separately; if
something is unavailable or cannot be assessed, report that limitation
instead of inventing a value or treating it as normal.

112
00:24:47,832 --> 00:25:09,216
Read both any single high-risk domain and the aggregate using that
same chart’s escalation instructions. Communicate the score with the
actual findings, time, change from baseline and clinical concern. The
school’s detailed NEWS2 scoring is taught in 7D; PEWS band details
belong to the designated pediatric teaching.

113
00:25:09,216 --> 00:25:29,904
Original process drill: a child has new work of breathing and a
behavior change. Identify the correct school PEWS reference, confirm
the age band, collect the chart’s required observations, then explain
how the result would be communicated and acted on. Do not substitute
an adult NEWS2 calculation.

114
00:25:29,904 --> 00:25:41,664
Use the school thirty-minute PAA rotation as a supervised practice
block, not a thirty-minute permission to delay transport. Divide roles
and rehearse the complete decision chain.

115
00:25:41,664 --> 00:25:53,544
Have the instructor introduce a change after movement. Stop the
current nonessential task, return to immediate threats, verify new
measurements and revise your plan aloud.

116
00:25:53,544 --> 00:26:05,016
Then produce your own PCR from the observed simulation and deliver a
concise transfer report. Use the documentation and communication
workshops already included in this week.

117
00:26:05,016 --> 00:26:18,000
For review, explain the reason for every priority and what finding
would change it. The aim is a reproducible safe assessment, not
memorizing our fictional narrative or a school quiz answer.
